<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wenceslaus III of Bohemia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1289–1306</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Ladislaus V of Hungary</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wenceslaus III was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> from 1301 to 1305, as Ladislaus V, and king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1305–1306. Son of Wenceslaus II, he was elected in Hungary after the extinction of the Árpád dynasty but lost ground to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-hungary">Charles Robert of Anjou</a> and handed his claim to Otto of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. His murder at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a> in 1306 ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-iii-of-bohemia.jpg" alt="Wenceslaus III of Bohemia" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Szilágyi Sándor · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vaclav3_pecet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
